{"type": "message", "message": "**Minimalist means less visual clutter—not less comfort.** In a small flat, it’s about choosing a complete, useful set of furniture rather than filling every corner.\n\n- **Simple shapes:** clean-lined furniture sized for the room.\n- **A restrained palette:** a few related colours, with wood and soft fabrics for warmth—not necessarily all white.\n- **Useful storage:** closed cupboards keep everyday essentials accessible but out of sight.\n- **Clear routes:** arrange furniture so doors, windows and the balcony remain easy to reach.\n- **A few personal touches:** favourite objects have space to stand out.\n\nFor your combined living room and kitchen, that could mean a defined seating area with a rug, a reachable table and a lamp, rather than scattered small pieces. **The goal is a calm, practical home—not an empty showroom.**", "conversationId": "2cdcdf9073eb49f49acaeebbed4561cd"}
